Part A
The system is a cross-platform task manager and calendar. The application is beign dessigned to allow users to crete task and schedule time and date for the task to be completed on.
The aplication's purpose is to help user better manage task and dates so that task can be done on time.
FUNCTIONAL
1.Task Creation: The system shall allow users to type and input custom text descriptions to create new to-do tasks.
2.Task Completion: The system shall provide a checkbox UI element for each task that users can toggle to mark the task as completed or incomplete.
3.Calendar View & Assignment: The system shall display an integrated calendar view allowing users to assign tasks to specific dates throughout the month.
4.Tagging & Categorization: The system shall allow users to create and assign custom tags or labels to organize and group tasks by category.
5.Recurring Tasks: The system shall support recurring task configurations (e.g., daily, weekly, monthly) that automatically regenerate tasks based on the specified repeat schedule.
6.Task Search: The system shall include a search feature that allows users to filter and locate tasks by matching keywords in task descriptions or tags.
7.User Authentication: The system shall require users to log in with an account to securely store and associate their task data in the cloud.
8.Cross-Device Data Sync: The system shall synchronize user task data across multiple client platforms (e.g., mobile phones, web, or desktop browsers).
9.Offline Data Persistence: The system shall permit users to view, create, and update tasks while disconnected from the internet, storing changes locally until a connection is re-established.
10.Task Deletion & Editing: The system shall allow users to edit existing task details or delete tasks from their list.
11.Theme Selection: The system shall allow users to customize the user interface theme (e.g., light mode, dark mode) and adjust font settings.
12.Automatic Conflict Resolution: The system shall detect and resolve data conflicts during synchronization when offline changes are merged with server data upon reconnecting.
NON-FUNCTIONAL
1.(Performance)Load Times: The application shall load and display the user’s primary task list within 2 seconds of launch under standard network conditions.
2.(Usability)Accessibility & Visual Customization: The system interface shall support adjustable themes (including high-contrast/dark modes) and scalable font sizes to accommodate user preferences and visual accessibility needs.
3.(Reliability)Offline Reliability: The local data storage system shall maintain 100% data integrity while operating offline, ensuring zero data loss during network disruptions.

Part B
Use Case 1: Create a Recurring Task with Tag
Use Case Name: Create Recurring Task with Tag

Actors: Registered User

Preconditions:

User is authenticated and logged into the application.

Application is running and displaying the main task management interface.

Main Flow:

User clicks or taps the "Add Task" button/input field.

User types in the task description.

User selects a date from the calendar view to assign to the task.

User assigns one or more custom tags/categories to the task.

User selects the "Repeat" option and sets the recurrence frequency (e.g., daily, weekly, monthly).

User saves the task.

The system creates the task, schedules the recurrence schedule, and displays it on the user's task list and calendar view.

Alternate Flows:

A1: Mandatory Field Missing (No Task Description)

In Step 6, if the user attempts to save without entering a task description, the system displays an error prompt ("Task description cannot be empty").

User enters a description and saves again.

The flow resumes at Step 7.

A2: Offline Creation

In Step 6, if the device is offline, the system saves the new task locally to local storage and updates the local UI immediately.

The system queues the sync event to upload to the server once network connectivity is restored.

Postconditions:

The task is successfully stored (locally and/or in the cloud) and visible in both the list view and calendar view on the chosen date.

Use Case 2: Search and Filter Tasks by Keyword/Tag
Use Case Name: Search and Filter Tasks

Actors: Registered User

Preconditions:

User is logged into the application.

One or more tasks exist in the user's account.

Main Flow:

User navigates to or clicks on the search bar in the top navigation area.

User types a keyword, phrase, or tag name into the search bar.

As the user types, the system dynamically filters the active task list to display only tasks matching the search criteria in their title, description, or assigned tags.

User selects or reviews the matching task results.

Alternate Flows:

A1: No Matching Results Found

In Step 3, if no tasks match the entered keyword or tag, the system displays a "No matching tasks found" message.

User clears or modifies the search query.

System restores the default task list view.

A2: Search Cleared

User clicks the "Clear Search" icon or deletes all text from the search bar.

System resets the display to show all tasks for the selected date or category.

Postconditions:

The user views the filtered subset of tasks corresponding to the search term without altering any stored task data.

Use Case 3: Synchronize Local Offline Changes with Cloud
Use Case Name: Synchronize Offline Changes with Cloud Database

Actors: Registered User, Sync Service (System Actor)

Preconditions:

User has performed offline actions (e.g., created, updated, completed, or deleted tasks) while disconnected.

The device transitions from offline to online status (network connectivity restored).

Main Flow:

The Sync Service detects an active internet connection.

The Sync Service reads all pending locally cached changes/events.

The system transmits the pending local updates to the central cloud database.

The server merges the updates and returns confirmation along with any new remote updates.

The application updates local storage and refreshes the UI to reflect the synchronized state.

Alternate Flows:

A1: Synchronization Conflict Detected

In Step 4, the server detects that a task modified locally was also modified on another device prior to reconnecting.

The system executes automatic conflict resolution rules (e.g., merging non-conflicting fields or retaining the latest timestamp).

The updated resolved state is saved locally and on the server.

A2: Network Drops During Sync

In Step 3 or 4, if the network connection fails before sync completion, the system halts the sync operation.

The system retains the pending changes in local storage.

The system waits until connection is re-established to restart the process at Step 1.

Postconditions:

Local and cloud databases are fully synchronized and contain identical task state information across all user devices.
